1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:04,290
Narrator: The Law Behind the Deal.

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:06,587
Narrator: Episode 8. The Buyer's Side

3
00:00:10,287 --> 00:00:15,366
Hans: Kito, my invoice is thirty days overdue. Two hundred thousand euros!

4
00:00:15,516 --> 00:00:19,010
Kito: Not until every machine works. I'm holding back the whole price!

5
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:27,107
Professor Lex: Careful, Kito. The buyer has duties too, and refusing to pay for good machines could put SafariTech in breach.

6
00:00:27,157 --> 00:00:33,987
Professor Lex: Under the CISG, the buyer has two core obligations: pay the price, and take delivery of the goods.

7
00:00:37,287 --> 00:00:45,450
Professor Lex: Paying includes the steps needed to make payment possible, like opening a letter of credit, or getting a bank guarantee.

8
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:52,378
Professor Lex: If no price was fixed, it is the price generally charged for such goods when the contract was made.

9
00:00:52,378 --> 00:00:55,848
Professor Lex: If the price depends on weight, use the net weight.

10
00:00:55,848 --> 00:01:03,371
Professor Lex: Place: the seller's place of business, or, if payment is against goods or documents, wherever they are handed over.

11
00:01:03,371 --> 00:01:12,712
Professor Lex: Time: when the seller places the goods or documents at the buyer's disposal. The seller can make payment a condition of handing them over.

12
00:01:12,712 --> 00:01:18,929
Professor Lex: The buyer may examine the goods before paying, unless the agreed payment method rules that out.

13
00:01:18,929 --> 00:01:23,751
Professor Lex: And the buyer must pay on the due date, without any request from the seller.

14
00:01:23,901 --> 00:01:28,254
Amara: That's why our bank asks importers to set up payment terms before the goods ship.

15
00:01:31,754 --> 00:01:34,573
Professor Lex: Taking delivery has two parts.

16
00:01:34,623 --> 00:01:45,451
Professor Lex: First, doing everything that can reasonably be expected so the seller can deliver. For example, sending a ship under FOB, or giving specifications.

17
00:01:45,501 --> 00:01:48,220
Professor Lex: Second, actually taking over the goods.

18
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:52,004
Captain Cargo: So if nobody collects me at the port, that's the buyer's breach!

19
00:01:52,054 --> 00:01:59,470
Professor Lex: Exactly. And even a buyer who rejects goods must look after them. We'll see that duty in the next episode.

20
00:02:02,970 --> 00:02:07,178
Professor Lex: If the buyer breaches, the seller's toolkit mirrors the buyer's.

21
00:02:07,228 --> 00:02:16,317
Professor Lex: Require the buyer to pay, take delivery, or perform its other obligations, unless the seller has chosen an inconsistent remedy.

22
00:02:16,317 --> 00:02:21,425
Professor Lex: Fix an additional period of reasonable length for the buyer to perform.

23
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:29,715
Professor Lex: Avoid the contract, for a fundamental breach, or if the buyer does not pay or take delivery within the additional period.

24
00:02:29,715 --> 00:02:34,882
Professor Lex: If the buyer fails to give specifications, the seller may supply them itself.

25
00:02:34,882 --> 00:02:38,597
Professor Lex: And claim damages, alongside any other remedy.

26
00:02:38,847 --> 00:02:48,752
Professor Lex: One limit: once the buyer has paid, the seller generally loses the right to avoid, unless it acts quickly about late performance or another breach.

27
00:02:48,802 --> 00:02:54,636
Hans: So I could fix a deadline, and then avoid the contract if SafariTech still doesn't pay.

28
00:02:58,135 --> 00:03:02,387
Hans: Next time, I'll keep ownership of my machines until I'm paid.

29
00:03:02,537 --> 00:03:09,598
Professor Lex: That is a retention of title clause, often called a Romalpa clause, after a famous English case.

30
00:03:09,648 --> 00:03:19,193
Professor Lex: A Dutch supplier sold aluminium foil to an English company, with a clause keeping ownership until payment. The buyer became insolvent.

31
00:03:19,243 --> 00:03:28,046
Professor Lex: The court held the supplier could recover its unsold foil, and the proceeds of resales held for it, ahead of the other creditors.

32
00:03:29,396 --> 00:03:37,550
Professor Lex: But remember: the CISG does not deal with ownership. Whether such a clause works depends on the national law that applies.

33
00:03:41,050 --> 00:03:42,764
Nia: So what should we do, Professor?

34
00:03:42,814 --> 00:03:50,085
Professor Lex: Pay for the three machines that conform. Holding back the whole price could itself be a fundamental breach.

35
00:03:50,135 --> 00:03:58,926
Professor Lex: For machine two, demand repair, or reduce the price. For machine five, ask for a substitute, and keep your damages claim.

36
00:03:59,076 --> 00:04:03,513
Kito: Fine. Pay for what works, and fight for the rest. Hans, the money's on its way.

37
00:04:03,563 --> 00:04:06,596
Hans: Danke! And I'll send a technician for machine two.

38
00:04:10,496 --> 00:04:14,590
The Auditor: Quick check. If the contract does not fix a price, what price is payable?

39
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:23,498
The Auditor: The price generally charged for such goods when the contract was made.

40
00:04:27,448 --> 00:04:30,134
The Auditor: And what is the effect of a Romalpa clause?

41
00:04:34,184 --> 00:04:36,868
The Auditor: The seller keeps ownership until it is paid.

42
00:04:37,018 --> 00:04:40,883
Professor Lex: Whether it works depends on the applicable national law.

43
00:04:44,432 --> 00:04:46,509
Nia: Pay the price and take delivery.

44
00:04:46,559 --> 00:04:49,305
Nia: Payment includes steps like a letter of credit.

45
00:04:49,355 --> 00:04:52,459
Nia: No price fixed? The general price at contract.

46
00:04:52,509 --> 00:04:54,596
Nia: Seller's remedies mirror the buyer's.

47
00:04:54,646 --> 00:04:56,973
Nia: Romalpa keeps title until payment.

48
00:04:57,023 --> 00:05:05,614
Professor Lex: Next time, things go really wrong: anticipatory breach, instalments, damages, and events beyond anyone's control.

49
00:05:05,664 --> 00:05:08,414
Captain Cargo: Uh oh. I hope nobody drops me in the ocean!

50
00:05:08,764 --> 00:05:11,685
Narrator: Next time: Episode 9. When Deals Break.

